{"task_id": "Mbpp/101", "solution": "```python\ndef kth_element(array: list[int], k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Retrieves the element at the k-th position (1-based index) from the given array.\n\n    Args:\n        array: The list of numbers to search within.\n        k: The 1-based index of the element to find.\n\n    Returns:\n        The element found at the k-th position.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If the input array is empty.\n        IndexError: If k is less than 1 or greater than the length of the array.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not array:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input array cannot be empty.\")\n    if not (1 <= k <= len(array)):\n        raise IndexError(f\"Index k ({k}) is out of bounds for array of length {len(array)}.\")\n    return array[k - 1]\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/101", "solution": "def kth_element(arr: list[int], k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the Kth smallest element in the input list arr using 1-based indexing.\n\n    Args:\n        arr: The list of integers to search within.\n        k: The 1-based index of the element to find.\n\n    Returns:\n        The Kth smallest element.\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: If 'arr' is not a list, 'k' is not an integer, or elements in 'arr' are not integers.\n        ValueError: If the input array 'arr' is empty.\n        IndexError: If 'k' is less than 1 or greater than the length of 'arr'.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(arr, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input 'arr' must be a list.\")\n    if not all(isinstance(x, int) for x in arr):\n        raise TypeError(\"All elements in 'arr' must be integers.\")\n    if not isinstance(k, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input 'k' must be an integer.\")\n\n    if not arr:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input array cannot be empty.\")\n    if k < 1 or k > len(arr):\n        raise IndexError(f\"k ({k}) is out of bounds for array of length {len(arr)}.\")\n\n    sorted_arr = sorted(arr)\n    return sorted_arr[k - 1]"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/100", "solution": "```python\ndef next_smallest_palindrome(n: int) -> int:\n    # Converts an integer to a list of its digits.\n    # Example: 123 -> [1, 2, 3]\n    def _int_to_list(num: int) -> list[int]:\n        return [int(d) for d in str(num)]\n\n    # Converts a list of digits to an integer.\n    # Example: [1, 2, 3] -> 123\n    def _list_to_int(digits_list: list[int]) -> int:\n        return int(\"\".join(map(str, digits_list)))\n\n    # Start from n + 1 to ensure the next smallest palindrome is strictly greater.\n    # This handles cases where n is already a palindrome.\n    current_num_val = n + 1\n    current_digits = _int_to_list(current_num_val)\n    length = len(current_digits)\n\n    mid = length // 2\n    \n    # Make a copy to generate the first palindrome candidate by mirroring the left half.\n    temp_digits = list(current_digits)\n    for k in range(mid):\n        temp_digits[length - 1 - k] = temp_digits[k]\n    \n    candidate_num = _list_to_int(temp_digits)\n    \n    # If this candidate palindrome is already greater than or equal to current_num_val,\n    # then it is the next smallest palindrome.\n    if candidate_num >= current_num_val:\n        return candidate_num\n    \n    # If the candidate palindrome is smaller, we need to increment the center part\n    # of the original number's digits (current_digits) and then re-mirror.\n    \n    carry = 1 # Initialize carry for incrementing\n    \n    # Handle the middle digit for odd length numbers first.\n    if length % 2 == 1:\n        s = current_digits[mid] + carry\n        current_digits[mid] = s % 10\n        carry = s // 10\n    \n    # Propagate carry through the left half (from right to left).\n    for k in range(mid - 1, -1, -1):\n        s = current_digits[k] + carry\n        current_digits[k] = s % 10\n        carry = s // 10\n        if carry == 0: break # Stop if no more carry\n    \n    # If carry remains after processing all relevant digits, it implies a length increase.\n    # For example, n=9 implies current_num_val=10, length=2.\n    # The incremented digits would result in [1] with carry=0.\n    # For n=9, (n+1)=10. Initial mirrored palindrome is 11. 11 >= 10. So it returns 11.\n    # For n=99, (n+1)=100. Initial mirrored palindrome is 101. 101 >= 100. So it returns 101.\n    # The path where `carry > 0` after increment logic for the left half:\n    # Example: If `current_digits` for some reason became `[9, 9]`.\n    # `mid=1`. No middle digit handled. `k=0` (left_half_end_idx). `s=9+1=10`. `current_digits[0]=0`. `carry=1`. Loop ends.\n    # Now `carry` is 1. This means new palindrome is `1` + `0`s + `1`.\n    if carry > 0:\n        return _list_to_int([1] + [0] * (length - 1) + [1])\n    \n    # Re-mirror the (now incremented) left half to form the final palindrome.\n    for k in range(mid):\n        current_digits[length - 1 - k] = current_digits[k]\n    \n    return _list_to_int(current_digits)\n```\n"}
