{"task_id": "Mbpp/119", "solution": "```python\ndef search(arr: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the single unique element in a sorted array.\n    All other elements appear exactly twice.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Handles the base case for a single-element array.\n    if len(arr) == 1:\n        return arr[0]\n\n    low = 0\n    high = len(arr) - 1\n\n    while low < high:\n        mid = (low + high) // 2\n\n        # Check if the middle element is part of a pair.\n        # `mid ^ 1` gives the index of the pair: mid+1 if mid is even, mid-1 if mid is odd.\n        if arr[mid] == arr[mid ^ 1]:\n            # The pair is found, so the unique element must be to the right.\n            # Advance 'low' past this complete pair.\n            low = mid + 2 if mid % 2 == 0 else mid + 1\n        else:\n            # The pair is not found, so the unique element is at 'mid' or to its left.\n            high = mid\n\n    # When low equals high, that index holds the unique element.\n    return arr[low]\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/119", "solution": "```python\ndef search(arr: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the element that appears only once in a sorted array where all other\n    elements appear exactly twice, using a binary search approach.\n\n    Args:\n        arr: A sorted list of integers where exactly one element appears once\n             and all other elements appear twice. The list is guaranteed to be\n             non-empty and contain a unique element.\n\n    Returns:\n        The integer that appears only once in the array.\n    \"\"\"\n    left, right = 0, len(arr) - 1\n\n    while left < right:\n        mid = (left + right) // 2\n\n        # Determine if the current mid element is part of a pair or potentially the unique element.\n        # If mid is an even index, its pair should be at mid + 1.\n        # If mid is an odd index, its pair should be at mid - 1.\n        \n        # Check if arr[mid] is part of a pair\n        is_part_of_pair = False\n        if mid % 2 == 0:  # mid is an even index\n            # Check if arr[mid] forms a pair with arr[mid + 1]\n            if mid + 1 < len(arr) and arr[mid] == arr[mid + 1]:\n                is_part_of_pair = True\n        else:  # mid is an odd index\n            # Check if arr[mid] forms a pair with arr[mid - 1]\n            if mid - 1 >= 0 and arr[mid] == arr[mid - 1]:\n                is_part_of_pair = True\n\n        if is_part_of_pair:\n            # If arr[mid] is part of a pair, the unique element must be in the right half.\n            # Move 'left' pointer past this pair.\n            # If mid was even, the pair is (mid, mid+1), so new 'left' starts at mid+2.\n            # If mid was odd, the pair is (mid-1, mid), so new 'left' starts at mid+1.\n            left = mid + 2 if mid % 2 == 0 else mid + 1\n        else:\n            # If arr[mid] is not part of a pair (i.e., it's either the unique element\n            # or part of a pair whose other half is before mid), the unique element\n            # must be at or to the left of mid.\n            right = mid\n\n    # When the loop terminates, 'left' and 'right' pointers will converge on the\n    # index of the unique element.\n    return arr[left]\n```"}
