{"task_id": "Mbpp/235", "solution": "def even_bit_set_number(number: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Sets all even-indexed bits (0, 2, 4, ...) of the given number to 1.\n    This function operates on non-negative integers.\n    \"\"\"\n    mask = 0\n    bit_pos = 0\n    while True:\n        current_bit_mask = 1 << bit_pos\n        if current_bit_mask > number and current_bit_mask > mask: # Stop if current_bit_mask is larger than number and current mask.\n            break\n        mask |= current_bit_mask\n        bit_pos += 2\n        \n    return number | mask"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/237", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Tuple, Any, Dict\n\ndef check_occurences(tuple_list: List[Tuple[Any, ...]]) -> Dict[Tuple[Any, ...], int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts the occurrences of unique (order-agnostic) tuples in a list.\n    It returns a dictionary mapping each normalized tuple to its frequency.\n    \"\"\"\n    occurrence_map: Dict[Tuple[Any, ...], int] = {}\n    for t in tuple_list:\n        # Normalize the tuple by sorting its elements to create a canonical representation.\n        # This ensures that tuples like (3, 1) and (1, 3) are treated as the same.\n        normalized_t = tuple(sorted(t))\n        occurrence_map[normalized_t] = occurrence_map.get(normalized_t, 0) + 1\n    return occurrence_map\n```"}
