{"task_id": "Mbpp/244", "solution": "```python\ndef next_Perfect_Square(number: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the smallest perfect square strictly greater than the given integer.\n    Assumes a non-negative integer input.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Calculate the integer part of the square root.\n    # For example, for 35, this is 5; for 36, this is 6.\n    integer_sqrt = int(number**0.5)\n\n    # The next perfect square will be the square of (integer_sqrt + 1).\n    # For 35, integer_sqrt is 5, so (5+1)^2 = 36.\n    # For 36, integer_sqrt is 6, so (6+1)^2 = 49.\n    next_root = integer_sqrt + 1\n    return next_root * next_root\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/244", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef next_Perfect_Square(number: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the smallest perfect square strictly greater than the input number.\n    For negative inputs, it returns 0, as 0 is the smallest non-negative perfect square.\n    \"\"\"\n    if number < 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # Find the integer square root (floor(sqrt(number))).\n    # math.isqrt is available in Python 3.8+ and is efficient for integers.\n    sqrt_val = math.isqrt(number)\n\n    # The next perfect square's root is simply one greater than the current integer square root.\n    next_sqrt_root = sqrt_val + 1\n\n    return next_sqrt_root * next_sqrt_root\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/245", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef max_sum(arr: List[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the maximum sum of a bitonic subsequence in an array.\n    A bitonic subsequence first strictly increases, then strictly decreases.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    if n == 1:\n        return arr[0]\n\n    def _calculate_lis_sum_internal(inner_arr: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n        \"\"\"Calculates the maximum sum of an increasing subsequence ending at each position.\"\"\"\n        inner_n = len(inner_arr)\n        lis_sum = [0] * inner_n\n        for i in range(inner_n):\n            lis_sum[i] = inner_arr[i]\n            for j in range(i):\n                if inner_arr[i] > inner_arr[j]:\n                    lis_sum[i] = max(lis_sum[i], lis_sum[j] + inner_arr[i])\n        return lis_sum\n\n    def _calculate_lds_sum_internal(inner_arr: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n        \"\"\"Calculates the maximum sum of a decreasing subsequence starting at each position.\"\"\"\n        inner_n = len(inner_arr)\n        lds_sum = [0] * inner_n\n        for i in range(inner_n - 1, -1, -1):\n            lds_sum[i] = inner_arr[i]\n            for j in range(inner_n - 1, i, -1):\n                if inner_arr[i] > inner_arr[j]:\n                    lds_sum[i] = max(lds_sum[i], lds_sum[j] + inner_arr[i])\n        return lds_sum\n\n    lis_sum = _calculate_lis_sum_internal(arr)\n    lds_sum = _calculate_lds_sum_internal(arr)\n\n    # Initialize max_sum with the maximum single element to correctly handle arrays\n    # where all bitonic sums might be negative.\n    max_sum = max(arr)\n\n    for i in range(n):\n        # `arr[i]` is the peak element. It's included in both LIS ending at `i`\n        # and LDS starting at `i`, so subtract `arr[i]` once to avoid double counting.\n        current_bitonic_sum = lis_sum[i] + lds_sum[i] - arr[i]\n        max_sum = max(max_sum, current_bitonic_sum)\n\n    return max_sum\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/245", "solution": "```python\ndef max_sum(arr: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum sum of a bitonic subsequence in the given array.\n    A sequence is bitonic if it is first increasing and then decreasing.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not arr:\n        return 0\n\n    n = len(arr)\n\n    # Calculate LIS sums for each element\n    # lis_sums[i] stores the sum of the longest increasing subsequence ending at arr[i]\n    lis_sums = list(arr)\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            if arr[i] > arr[j] and lis_sums[i] < lis_sums[j] + arr[i]:\n                lis_sums[i] = lis_sums[j] + arr[i]\n\n    # Calculate LDS sums for each element\n    # lds_sums[i] stores the sum of the longest decreasing subsequence starting at arr[i]\n    lds_sums = list(arr)\n    for i in range(n - 2, -1, -1):\n        for j in range(n - 1, i, -1):\n            if arr[i] > arr[j] and lds_sums[i] < lds_sums[j] + arr[i]:\n                lds_sums[i] = lds_sums[j] + arr[i]\n\n    max_bitonic_sum = 0\n    # Combine LIS and LDS sums to find the maximum bitonic subsequence sum\n    for i in range(n):\n        # The current element arr[i] is the peak of the bitonic subsequence.\n        # It's included in both lis_sums[i] and lds_sums[i], so we subtract it once.\n        current_bitonic_sum = lis_sums[i] + lds_sums[i] - arr[i]\n        if current_bitonic_sum > max_bitonic_sum:\n            max_bitonic_sum = current_bitonic_sum\n            \n    return max_bitonic_sum\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/247", "solution": "```python\ndef lps(s):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the length of the longest palindromic subsequence in the given string.\n    Uses dynamic programming with O(n^2) time and space complexity.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(s)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # dp[i][j] stores the length of the LPS in substring s[i..j]\n    dp = [[0 for _ in range(n)] for _ in range(n)]\n\n    # Every single character is a palindrome of length 1\n    for i in range(n):\n        dp[i][i] = 1\n\n    # Fill the DP table for substrings of increasing length\n    for L in range(2, n + 1):\n        for i in range(n - L + 1):\n            j = i + L - 1\n            if s[i] == s[j]:\n                # If characters match, extend the palindrome from inner substring\n                dp[i][j] = 2 + dp[i + 1][j - 1] if L > 2 else 2\n            else:\n                # If characters don't match, take max from removing s[i] or s[j]\n                dp[i][j] = max(dp[i][j - 1], dp[i + 1][j])\n    return dp[0][n - 1]\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/247", "solution": "```python\ndef lps(s: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the length of the longest palindromic subsequence (LPS) for the input string.\n    This uses a dynamic programming approach to ensure efficiency.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(s)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # dp[i][j] stores the length of the LPS of substring s[i...j]\n    dp = [[0] * n for _ in range(n)]\n\n    # Every single character is a palindrome of length 1\n    for i in range(n):\n        dp[i][i] = 1\n\n    # Fill the dp table for subsequences of length 2 to n\n    for length in range(2, n + 1):\n        for i in range(n - length + 1):\n            j = i + length - 1\n            if s[i] == s[j]:\n                dp[i][j] = 2 + (dp[i + 1][j - 1] if length > 2 else 0)\n            else:\n                dp[i][j] = max(dp[i + 1][j], dp[i][j - 1])\n\n    return dp[0][n - 1]\n```"}
