{"task_id": "Mbpp/282", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Union, Any\n\ndef sub_list(list1: List[Union[int, float]], list2: List[Union[int, float]]) -> List[Union[int, float]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Subtracts two lists element-wise.\n\n    Args:\n        list1: The first list of numbers.\n        list2: The second list of numbers to be subtracted.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new list containing the differences.\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: If either input is not a list, or if any element is not a number.\n        ValueError: If the input lists do not have the same length.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(list1, list) or not isinstance(list2, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"Both inputs must be lists.\")\n\n    if len(list1) != len(list2):\n        raise ValueError(\"Input lists must have the same length.\")\n\n    result = []\n    for i, (a, b) in enumerate(zip(list1, list2)):\n        if not isinstance(a, (int, float)):\n            raise TypeError(f\"Element at index {i} in the first list is not a number: {a}\")\n        if not isinstance(b, (int, float)):\n            raise TypeError(f\"Element at index {i} in the second list is not a number: {b}\")\n        result.append(a - b)\n\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/283", "solution": "```python\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef validate(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks whether the frequency of each digit in the integer is less than or equal to the digit itself.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(number, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input must be an integer.\")\n\n    abs_number = abs(number)\n    \n    # Handle the special case for number 0 explicitly as str(0) is \"0\" and int(d) for d in \"0\" is [0]\n    # and Counter({0:1}) will be generated, which is then processed correctly by the loop.\n    # The original design code snippet has `if number == 0: return {0: 1}` inside DigitCounter,\n    # but the loop `while temp_num > 0` would not capture 0 itself if `abs_number` starts at 0.\n    # So using `str(abs_number)` for robust digit extraction, including for `0`.\n    \n    digit_str = str(abs_number)\n    digit_frequencies = Counter(int(d) for d in digit_str)\n\n    for digit, frequency in digit_frequencies.items():\n        if frequency > digit:\n            return False\n\n    return True\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/283", "solution": "```python\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef validate(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the frequency of each digit in 'number' is less than or\n    equal to the digit's value. Assumes input is a non-negative integer.\n    \"\"\"\n    if number < 0:\n        # The problem statement implies non-negative digits for frequency check.\n        # Negative numbers do not logically satisfy the condition in a straightforward way.\n        return False\n\n    if number == 0:\n        # Digit 0 appears once, frequency 1. 1 <= 0 is False.\n        return False\n\n    number_str = str(number)\n    digit_frequencies = {int(digit): count for digit, count in Counter(number_str).items()}\n\n    for digit_val, frequency in digit_frequencies.items():\n        if frequency > digit_val:\n            return False\n    return True\n```"}
