{"task_id": "Mbpp/468", "solution": "```python\nclass MaxProductSolver:\n    def find_max_product(self, arr: list[int]) -> int:\n        n = len(arr)\n        if n == 0:\n            return 1\n\n        dp = list(arr) # dp[i] stores the max product of an increasing subsequence ending at arr[i].\n\n        for i in range(n):\n            for j in range(i):\n                if arr[i] > arr[j]:\n                    current_product = dp[j] * arr[i]\n                    # Update dp[i] if a greater product is found by extending a previous subsequence.\n                    if current_product > dp[i]:\n                        dp[i] = current_product\n        \n        # The maximum product could be positive, negative, or zero based on the array elements.\n        return max(dp)\n\ndef max_product(arr: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Finds the maximum product of an increasing subsequence within the given array. Returns 1 if the array is empty.\"\"\"\n    solver = MaxProductSolver()\n    return solver.find_max_product(arr)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/468", "solution": "```python\ndef max_product(arr: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the maximum product of an increasing subsequence in an array.\n    Uses dynamic programming for an O(n^2) time complexity.\"\"\"\n\n    if not arr:\n        return 0\n\n    n = len(arr)\n    dp = [0] * n\n\n    # Initialize dp array: each element itself is an increasing subsequence.\n    for i in range(n):\n        dp[i] = arr[i]\n\n    # Fill dp array by extending previous increasing subsequences.\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            if arr[i] > arr[j]:\n                dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] * arr[i])\n\n    # The maximum product is the largest value found in the dp array.\n    return max(dp)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/470", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import Tuple, Union\n\ndef add_pairwise(input_tuple: Tuple[Union[int, float], ...]) -> Tuple[Union[int, float], ...]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the pairwise addition of neighboring elements of the given tuple.\n    Returns an empty tuple if the input tuple has fewer than two elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(input_tuple) < 2:\n        return ()\n\n    result = []\n    for i in range(len(input_tuple) - 1):\n        result.append(input_tuple[i] + input_tuple[i+1])\n\n    return tuple(result)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/470", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import Tuple, Union\n\ndef add_pairwise(input_tuple: Tuple[Union[int, float], ...]) -> Tuple[Union[int, float], ...]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of neighboring elements in the input_tuple.\n\n    Args:\n        input_tuple: A tuple of numeric values (integers or floats).\n\n    Returns:\n        A new tuple containing the pairwise sums.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If input_tuple is not a tuple, contains non-numeric elements,\n                    or has fewer than two elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(input_tuple, tuple):\n        raise ValueError(\"Input must be a tuple.\")\n    if len(input_tuple) < 2:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input tuple must contain at least two elements.\")\n\n    result = []\n    for i in range(len(input_tuple) - 1):\n        num1 = input_tuple[i]\n        num2 = input_tuple[i+1]\n        if not isinstance(num1, (int, float)) or not isinstance(num2, (int, float)):\n            raise ValueError(\"All elements in the input tuple must be numeric.\")\n        result.append(num1 + num2)\n    return tuple(result)\n```\n"}
