{"task_id": "Mbpp/472", "solution": "```python\ndef check_Consecutive(numbers: list) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a list of numbers contains consecutive integers after sorting and processing.\n    Handles empty lists, single-element lists, and correctly identifies duplicates as non-consecutive.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(numbers) <= 1:\n        return True\n\n    # Convert all numbers to integers for consistent comparison.\n    processed_numbers = []\n    for num in numbers:\n        processed_numbers.append(int(num))\n\n    # Sort the numbers and remove duplicates to get the unique sequence for checking.\n    unique_sorted_numbers = sorted(list(set(processed_numbers)))\n\n    # If removing duplicates changed the list's length, the original sequence was not strictly consecutive.\n    if len(unique_sorted_numbers) != len(processed_numbers):\n        return False\n\n    # Iterate and check if each subsequent number is exactly one greater than the preceding.\n    for i in range(len(unique_sorted_numbers) - 1):\n        if unique_sorted_numbers[i] + 1 != unique_sorted_numbers[i+1]:\n            return False\n            \n    return True\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/472", "solution": "```python\ndef check_Consecutive(numbers):\n    \"\"\"Checks if the given list contains any consecutive numbers (n, n+1).\n    Input is assumed to be a list of numbers, with duplicates handled.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(numbers) < 2:\n        return False\n\n    sorted_unique_numbers = sorted(list(set(numbers)))\n\n    for i in range(len(sorted_unique_numbers) - 1):\n        if sorted_unique_numbers[i] + 1 == sorted_unique_numbers[i+1]:\n            return True\n            \n    return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/473", "solution": "```python\ndef tuple_intersection(list1: list[tuple], list2: list[tuple]) -> set[tuple]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the intersection of two lists of tuples, ignoring element order within tuples.\n    Normalizes tuples by sorting their elements before performing set intersection.\n    \"\"\"\n    normalized_set1 = {tuple(sorted(t)) for t in list1}\n    normalized_set2 = {tuple(sorted(t)) for t in list2}\n    return normalized_set1.intersection(normalized_set2)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/473", "solution": "```python\ndef tuple_intersection(list1: list[tuple], list2: list[tuple]) -> set[tuple]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the intersection of two lists of tuples, treating tuples with the same\n    elements as identical regardless of their internal order.\n    Returns a set of tuples representing the order-agnostic intersection.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Canonicalize tuples using frozensets for order-agnostic comparison.\n    # This leverages frozenset's hashability for efficient set operations.\n    canonical_set1 = {frozenset(t) for t in list1}\n    canonical_set2 = {frozenset(t) for t in list2}\n\n    intersection_canonical = canonical_set1.intersection(canonical_set2)\n\n    # Convert frozensets back to sorted tuples for a consistent final output.\n    result_set = {tuple(sorted(list(fs))) for fs in intersection_canonical}\n\n    return result_set\n```"}
