{"task_id": "Mbpp/589", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef perfect_squares(start: int, end: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds all perfect squares between 'start' and 'end' (inclusive).\n\n    Args:\n        start: The lower bound of the range.\n        end: The upper bound of the range.\n\n    Returns:\n        A list of perfect squares within the specified range.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If start or end are not integers, or if start > end.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(start, int) or not isinstance(end, int):\n        raise ValueError(\"Both 'start' and 'end' must be integers.\")\n    if start > end:\n        raise ValueError(\"'start' cannot be greater than 'end'.\")\n\n    perfect_squares_list = []\n    \n    # Perfect squares are non-negative, so adjust start if it's negative for square root calculation.\n    # The actual_start for iteration will be based on 0 if original start is negative.\n    actual_start_for_sqrt = max(0, start)\n\n    # Calculate the smallest integer whose square is >= actual_start_for_sqrt\n    # and the largest integer whose square is <= end\n    current_num = math.ceil(math.sqrt(actual_start_for_sqrt))\n    max_sqrt_end = math.floor(math.sqrt(end))\n\n    while current_num <= max_sqrt_end:\n        square = current_num * current_num\n        # Ensure the square is within the original requested range (start and end)\n        # This check is important if the original 'start' was negative and 'actual_start_for_sqrt' became 0.\n        if start <= square <= end:\n            perfect_squares_list.append(square)\n        current_num += 1\n        \n    return perfect_squares_list\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/589", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef perfect_squares(num1: int, num2: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds all perfect squares within the inclusive range [num1, num2].\n    Returns a list of perfect squares in ascending order.\n    \"\"\"\n    if num1 > num2:\n        return []\n\n    squares = []\n    # Start checking from the square root of the lower bound, ensuring non-negative input for math.sqrt.\n    start_sqrt = math.ceil(math.sqrt(max(0, num1)))\n    # Calculate the upper bound for square roots, ensuring non-negative input for math.sqrt.\n    end_sqrt = math.floor(math.sqrt(max(0, num2)))\n\n    for i in range(int(start_sqrt), int(end_sqrt) + 1):\n        square = i * i\n        if num1 <= square <= num2:\n            squares.append(square)\n    return squares\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/590", "solution": "import math\n\ndef polar_rect(r, theta):\n    \"\"\"\n    Converts polar coordinates (radius, angle in radians) to rectangular coordinates (x, y).\n\n    Args:\n        r (float or int): The radius of the polar coordinate. Must be non-negative.\n        theta (float or int): The angle of the polar coordinate in radians.\n\n    Returns:\n        tuple: A tuple (x, y) of floats representing the rectangular coordinates.\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: If r or theta are not valid numeric types.\n        ValueError: If r is negative.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(r, (int, float)) or not isinstance(theta, (int, float)):\n        raise TypeError(\"Radius and angle must be numeric types.\")\n    if r < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Radius cannot be negative.\")\n\n    x = r * math.cos(theta)\n    y = r * math.sin(theta)\n    return (x, y)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/590", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef polar_rect(r: float, theta: float) -> tuple[float, float]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Converts polar coordinates (r, theta) to rectangular coordinates (x, y).\n    The angle theta is expected in radians.\n    \"\"\"\n    x = r * math.cos(theta)\n    y = r * math.sin(theta)\n    return (x, y)\n```"}
