{"task_id": "Mbpp/597", "solution": "def find_kth(arr1: list[int], arr2: list[int], k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Finds the k-th smallest element from two sorted arrays.\n    This function uses a binary search approach for optimal time complexity.\"\"\"\n    len1, len2 = len(arr1), len(arr2)\n\n    if not (1 <= k <= len1 + len2):\n        raise ValueError(\"k is out of bounds\")\n\n    # Ensure arr1 is the shorter array to optimize the binary search range.\n    if len1 > len2:\n        return find_kth(arr2, arr1, k)\n\n    # The search space for the partition in arr1.\n    # mid1 must be at least max(0, k - len2) and at most min(len1, k).\n    low, high = max(0, k - len2), min(len1, k)\n\n    while low <= high:\n        mid1 = (low + high) // 2  # Partition point in arr1\n        mid2 = k - mid1           # Corresponding partition point in arr2\n\n        # Elements immediately to the left and right of the partitions.\n        # Use negative/positive infinity for boundary conditions to simplify comparisons.\n        L1 = arr1[mid1 - 1] if mid1 > 0 else float('-inf')\n        R1 = arr1[mid1] if mid1 < len1 else float('inf')\n        L2 = arr2[mid2 - 1] if mid2 > 0 else float('-inf')\n        R2 = arr2[mid2] if mid2 < len2 else float('inf')\n\n        # Check if the partitions are correct (L1 <= R2 and L2 <= R1).\n        # If true, the k-th element is the maximum of the elements to the left of the partitions.\n        if L1 <= R2 and L2 <= R1:\n            return max(L1, L2)\n        elif L1 > R2:  # Partition in arr1 is too far right, move left.\n            high = mid1 - 1\n        else:  # Partition in arr1 is too far left (L2 > R1), move right.\n            low = mid1 + 1"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/597", "solution": "```python\ndef find_kth(arr1: list[int], arr2: list[int], k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the k-th smallest element from two sorted arrays using binary search.\n    Assumes k is valid and arrays are sorted in ascending order.\n    \"\"\"\n    len1, len2 = len(arr1), len(arr2)\n\n    # Ensure arr1 is the shorter array for optimized binary search.\n    if len1 > len2:\n        arr1, arr2 = arr2, arr1\n        len1, len2 = len2, len1\n\n    # Binary search for the split point in arr1 (partition1).\n    # partition1 elements are taken from arr1, and partition2 = k - partition1 from arr2.\n    # The search range for partition1 is determined by ensuring partition1 and partition2 are non-negative\n    # and do not exceed the lengths of their respective arrays.\n    low, high = max(0, k - len2), min(k, len1)\n\n    while low <= high:\n        partition1 = (low + high) // 2\n        partition2 = k - partition1\n\n        # Determine elements at the boundaries of the partitions.\n        # Use float('-inf') for an empty left partition and float('inf') for an empty right partition.\n        max_left_arr1 = float('-inf') if partition1 == 0 else arr1[partition1 - 1]\n        min_right_arr1 = float('inf') if partition1 == len1 else arr1[partition1]\n\n        max_left_arr2 = float('-inf') if partition2 == 0 else arr2[partition2 - 1]\n        min_right_arr2 = float('inf') if partition2 == len2 else arr2[partition2]\n\n        # Check if the current partition is correct.\n        # This occurs when max element of left partitions <= min element of right partitions.\n        if max_left_arr1 <= min_right_arr2 and max_left_arr2 <= min_right_arr1:\n            # The k-th smallest element is the maximum of the elements at the end of the left partitions.\n            return max(max_left_arr1, max_left_arr2)\n        elif max_left_arr1 > min_right_arr2:\n            # Partition1 is too large; reduce its size by moving the high pointer left.\n            high = partition1 - 1\n        else:  # max_left_arr2 > min_right_arr1\n            # Partition1 is too small; increase its size by moving the low pointer right.\n            low = partition1 + 1\n\n    # This part of the code should not be reached if 'k' is a valid 1-indexed position\n    # within the combined range of the two arrays. The algorithm guarantees a return\n    # within the loop under such valid input conditions.\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/598", "solution": "```python\ndef count_digits(num: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Counts the number of digits in an integer.\"\"\"\n    if num == 0:\n        return 1\n    return len(str(abs(num)))\n\ndef get_digits(num: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"Extracts individual digits from an integer into a list.\"\"\"\n    return [int(d) for d in str(abs(num))]\n\ndef armstrong_number(num: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the given integer is an Armstrong number.\n    An Armstrong number equals the sum of its digits, each raised to the power of the number of digits.\n    \"\"\"\n    num_digits = count_digits(num)\n    digits = get_digits(num)\n\n    sum_of_powers = 0\n    for digit in digits:\n        sum_of_powers += digit ** num_digits\n\n    return sum_of_powers == num\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/598", "solution": "```python\ndef armstrong_number(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a given number is an Armstrong number.\n    An Armstrong number equals the sum of its digits, each raised to the power of the number of digits.\n    \"\"\"\n    num_str = str(number)\n    num_digits = len(num_str)\n    sum_of_powers = 0\n\n    for digit_char in num_str:\n        digit = int(digit_char)\n        sum_of_powers += digit ** num_digits\n\n    return sum_of_powers == number\n```"}
