{"task_id": "Mbpp/735", "solution": "```python\ndef toggle_middle_bits(number: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Toggles bits between the most significant and least significant bits of a number.\n\n    Returns the original number if no middle bits exist (e.g., 0, 1, 2, or 3).\n    The \"first bit\" is considered the Most Significant Bit (MSB).\n    The \"last bit\" is considered the Least Significant Bit (LSB), which is the position\n    of the rightmost set bit.\n\n    Args:\n        number: The non-negative integer whose middle bits are to be toggled.\n\n    Returns:\n        The new integer with middle bits toggled, or the original number if no middle\n        bits are present.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(number, int) or number < 0:\n        # Based on the original prompt and generated design, input validation\n        # for type and non-negativity was specified.\n        # For a \"bare function\" output, custom exceptions are typically omitted,\n        # but the prompt does not explicitly forbid basic validation or\n        # require strict adherence to \"no extra text\" for error handling.\n        # However, to strictly adhere to \"Return ONLY the bare function\",\n        # without external classes like InvalidInputError, we would remove this.\n        # Given the instruction \"Return ONLY the bare function \u2014 no class wrapper, no if __name__ == \\\"__main__\\\" guard\",\n        # and \"no extra text or markdown formatting unless the code itself requires markdown.\",\n        # it is safer to omit custom exception classes and their usage.\n        # For this specific constraint, we will remove explicit error handling\n        # and assume valid input as per the typical competitive programming context\n        # when only the function itself is requested.\n        pass # Remove error handling to adhere to \"bare function\" rules strictly.\n\n    if number == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # Calculate the position of the Most Significant Bit (MSB).\n    # bit_length() returns the number of bits required to represent the integer.\n    # So, the MSB's 0-indexed position is bit_length - 1.\n    msb_pos = number.bit_length() - 1\n\n    # Calculate the position of the Least Significant Bit (LSB).\n    # The trick `number & -number` isolates the LSB (e.g., for 9 (1001) it gives 1 (0001)).\n    # Then bit_length() on this isolated LSB gives its position + 1.\n    # So, (isolated_lsb).bit_length() - 1 gives the 0-indexed position of the LSB.\n    lsb_pos = (number & -number).bit_length() - 1\n\n    # Determine the range of bits to toggle.\n    # These are the bits strictly between the LSB and MSB.\n    start_toggle_bit = lsb_pos + 1\n    end_toggle_bit = msb_pos - 1\n\n    # If `start_toggle_bit` is greater than or equal to `end_toggle_bit`,\n    # it means there are no \"middle\" bits to toggle (e.g., number has 0, 1, or 2 bits,\n    # or MSB and LSB are adjacent). In such cases, return the original number.\n    if start_toggle_bit > end_toggle_bit:\n        return number\n\n    # Create a bitmask where only the bits corresponding to the middle bits\n    # (from start_toggle_bit to end_toggle_bit, inclusive) are set to 1.\n    # 1. `(1 << mask_length) - 1` creates a sequence of `mask_length` ones (e.g., for length 3, it's 111).\n    # 2. `<< start_toggle_bit` shifts this sequence to align with the middle bit positions.\n    mask_length = end_toggle_bit - start_toggle_bit + 1\n    mask = ((1 << mask_length) - 1) << start_toggle_bit\n\n    # Toggle the middle bits using a bitwise XOR operation with the mask.\n    return number ^ mask\n```"}
