{"task_id": "Mbpp/759", "solution": "```python\ndef is_decimal(s: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the string is a decimal number with at most two decimal places.\n    It returns True for valid formats, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not s: # BR4: The string must not be empty.\n        return False\n\n    start_idx = 0\n    if s[0] == '+' or s[0] == '-':\n        start_idx = 1\n\n    num_str = s[start_idx:]\n\n    if not num_str: # Handles inputs like \"+\", \"-\" which are invalid.\n        return False\n    \n    if num_str.endswith('.'): # Invalid for \"123.\"\n        return False\n\n    parts = num_str.split('.')\n\n    if len(parts) > 2: # \"1.2.3\"\n        return False\n\n    integer_part = parts[0]\n    decimal_part = \"\"\n    if len(parts) == 2:\n        decimal_part = parts[1]\n\n    # At least one part (integer or decimal) must contain digits. Handles \".\"\n    if not integer_part and not decimal_part:\n        return False\n\n    if integer_part and not integer_part.isdigit():\n        return False\n\n    if decimal_part and not decimal_part.isdigit():\n        return False\n\n    if len(decimal_part) > 2:\n        return False\n\n    return True\n```"}
